QUESTIONS = [
    {"id": "q-notation", "at": {"node": "section-II-A"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"What do $\beta$, $\tau$, $\alpha$, the bars and the zeros mean?",
     "a": r"$\beta$, between 0 and 1, is the fraction of force or stress relaxed after a long hold at fixed deformation: 0 is elastic, 1 a Maxwell fluid. $\tau=\eta/\beta k$ is the relaxation time. A bar marks a reference quantity, $\bar L$ or $\bar g_{ij}(t)$, which evolves; a zero marks a rest quantity, $\bar L^0$ or $\bar g^0_{ij}$, which is fixed; $g_{ij}$ is the actual metric. $\alpha$ is the truss stiffness ratio $\sqrt{k_2/2k_1}$, but also labels generalized coordinates $\lambda_\alpha$ and a mixing term $\alpha(x,t)$.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-II-A-p1", "sec-sls_von_Mises_truss-p1", "section-IV-A-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-reformulation", "at": {"node": "section-II-A"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Is the reference length a new physical assumption?",
     "a": r"No. For the standard linear solid it is an exact regrouping of the springs' rest lengths and the dashpot's length, and the paper calls its equations a mere reformulation of familiar viscoelastic dynamics. What it adds is a variable that fast deformations leave unchanged, so the element is an ordinary spring at every instant, and stability questions become geometric questions about where the reference lengths have moved.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-II-A-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-intermediate", "at": {"node": "sec-sls_von_Mises_truss"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why must the truss at intermediate stiffness snap back, rather than settle in its acquired state?",
     "a": r"After release the reference point keeps moving, towards a stationary mix of the current lengths and the rest lengths. A stationary state would have to be an equilibrium of the truss at its rest lengths, and at this stiffness the only stable one is the original position. So the inverted state cannot become stationary, its stability is lost at some time, and the truss snaps back.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-sls_von_Mises_truss-p6", "app-1d_sls_systems-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-kernel", "at": {"node": "section-IV-A"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why does only $\beta$, and not the shape of the memory kernel, decide stability?",
     "a": r"With a scalar kernel the reference metric relaxes towards $\bar g^{\rm stat}=\beta g+(1-\beta)\bar g^0$ whatever the kernel's shape; the kernel sets only how fast it gets there. Stationary states and their stability depend on that endpoint, hence on $\beta$ alone, while the kernel affects the time it takes to reach an instability. That is why the exponential kernel of the standard linear solid suffices.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-IV-A-p1", "section-IV-A-p2", "section-II-A-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-collinear", "at": {"node": "sec-stationary_states"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why does a stable shape reached abruptly from rest not creep at all, although its stresses relax?",
     "a": r"Starting from rest, the reference metric moves along the straight line from the rest metric towards the held configuration metric, so the elastic strain only shrinks by a common factor $1-\beta+\beta\phi(t)$. Forces that vanished at the start keep vanishing, and the Hessian stays a mix of the initial positive one and a positive stiffness term. The configuration therefore remains the closest admissible one throughout.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-stationary_states-p3", "sec-stationary_states-p5"]},
    {"id": "q-saddle", "at": {"node": "sec-stationary_states"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Can relaxation permanently stabilize a shape that was unstable at rest?",
     "a": r"Only if it is an extremal of the rest system, such as a saddle point. The stationary Hessian is $(1-\beta)$ times the rest one plus a positive term, so a saddle can become stable indefinitely, the more easily the closer $\beta$ is to 1. A shape that is not a rest extremal cannot be stationary, so any stability it acquires must eventually be lost.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-stationary_states-p2", "sec-transient_stability_loss-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-incompressible", "at": {"node": "sec-stationary_states"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why do the continuum claims need incompressibility?",
     "a": r"The proofs use a scalar metric memory kernel, so that relaxation moves every component of the reference metric towards the same mix of current and rest metrics. A compressible isotropic material has a second kernel $\psi$, which mixes components through changes of volume and breaks this collinearity. The paper states that the results still hold approximately, deviating less the closer the material is to incompressible; it does not estimate the deviation.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-III-D-p4", "section-IV-A-p1", "sec-stationary_states-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-isotropy", "at": {"node": "section-III-D"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Isotropic with respect to which metric, and does the choice matter?",
     "a": r"Rotations of a body described by metrics are defined only by a chosen metric. The paper takes the stiffness and memory kernel isotropic with respect to the rest reference metric, because isotropy reflects the material's composition, which the rest metric encodes. A material isotropic about the instantaneous reference metric is possible in principle and would need a different stiffness tensor; the paper does not treat it.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-III-D-p1", "section-III-D-p2"]},
    {"id": "q-held", "at": {"node": "sec-transient_stability_loss"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Must a popper be held inverted for it to snap back late?",
     "a": r"The argument fixes only that a popper which snaps a finite time after inversion had acquired its stability, since a shape stable at rest and reached from rest never loses it. How it was acquired is left open: holding it inverted long enough is the presumable route. Conversely, if the inverted shape was unstable at rest, no holding or other deformation keeps it inverted for good.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-transient_stability_loss-p2", "sec-transient_stability_loss-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-experiments", "at": {"node": "sec-transient_stability_loss"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Does the paper compare its predictions with experiments on poppers?",
     "a": r"Not in this text. The abstract mentions quantitative agreement with experiments on silicone rubber shells, but the popper section applies the stability rules qualitatively, and its figure shows a simulated cone ($\beta=0.3$, $\nu=0.47$) with flip times against thickness, noting a similar phase plot in earlier work and in a companion paper by the same authors. Measured data and a direct comparison are not shown here.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-transient_stability_loss-p1", "fig-hopper_popper"]},
    {"id": "q-single", "at": {"node": "section-V"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why does a single viscoelastic element show none of these instabilities?",
     "a": r"A single element, or any purely one-dimensional system, has a trivial set of admissible states, so its elastic energy has a single minimum about any reference length. Multistability, and with it acquired and lost stability, needs a sufficiently curved set of admissible states, as in the truss. In continua the Euclidean metrics form a non-convex set, which supplies the curvature and can also leave residual stress.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-V-p3", "fig-sls_von_mises_truss_lengths_space"]},
    {"id": "q-growth", "at": {"node": "section-V"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Could the rest metric itself change, as in growth or plasticity?",
     "a": r"In this theory it is constant. The paper suggests that letting it evolve, driven externally or by further constitutive relations, would give a covariant elasto-visco-plastic theory for growing tissue or plastic flow, in which fast growth could be masked by the slow relaxation of the reference metric. It does not develop this, and says its applicability remains to be shown.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-V-p4"]},
]

BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-spring-dashpot", "after": "section-I-p1", "title": "Spring–dashpot models of creep and stress relaxation", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>A dashpot is a piston in a viscous fluid: its force is proportional to its rate of extension, $F=\eta\dot x$, while a spring's force is proportional to its extension, $F=kx$. Combining the two gives the simplest models of viscoelasticity. In the Maxwell model a spring and a dashpot sit in series: held at fixed extension, the force decays exponentially to zero with time $\eta/k$ (stress relaxation), and under constant load it extends without limit, as a fluid flows. In the Kelvin–Voigt model they sit in parallel: under constant load the extension approaches its final value gradually (creep), but a sudden stretch meets infinite resistance. The standard linear solid adds a spring in parallel to a Maxwell element: it responds at once like a spring, relaxes only part of the force, and creeps to a finite extension, as cross-linked elastomers do. The fraction of force it relaxes is the paper's $\beta$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-hereditary", "after": "section-I-p3", "title": "Hereditary integrals and the memory kernel", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>Linear viscoelasticity rests on Boltzmann's superposition principle: the stress now is the sum of the responses to all past strain increments, each weighted by how long ago it happened. For one strain component, $\sigma(t)=\int_{-\infty}^{t}G(t-s)\,\dot\varepsilon(s)\,ds$, where the relaxation function $G$ starts at the instantaneous modulus and decays to the equilibrium modulus of a solid, or to zero for a fluid. Equivalently, the stress is an elastic term plus an integral of the past strain against a memory kernel, the derivative of $G$. Such laws fit measured creep and relaxation curves and can be used in numerical simulations, but they leave the state of the material implicit: to know its present response one needs its whole history. For questions of stability the paper replaces that history by one evolving geometric object, the reference length or reference metric.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-snap", "after": "sec-sls_von_Mises_truss-p1", "title": "Elastic stability, bistability and snap-through", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A structure in quasi-static equilibrium sits at a stationary point of its elastic energy $E(q)$, where the generalized forces $-\partial E/\partial q$ vanish. The equilibrium is stable when it is a local minimum, which for a smooth energy means that the Hessian $\partial^2E/\partial q_a\partial q_b$ is positive definite; a maximum or a saddle is unstable. A bistable structure has two minima, like a shallow arch or a shell that can be inverted. When a slowly varied parameter, such as a load, deforms the energy so that a minimum merges with a neighbouring saddle, the lowest eigenvalue of the Hessian reaches zero and the minimum disappears. The structure then jumps dynamically to the remaining minimum, releasing stored energy: snap-through. The von Mises truss, two bars meeting at a loaded point, is the textbook example of elastic stability and snap-through. In the paper the slowly varied parameter is the set of reference lengths, moved by relaxation.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-metric", "after": "sec-metric_linear_viscoelasticity-p1", "title": "Metric tensors and strain in elasticity", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>Label each material point by coordinates $x^i$ that move with it. A configuration places the point at $\mathbf r(x)$ in space, and the squared distance between neighbouring points is $g_{ij}dx^idx^j$ with $g_{ij}=\partial_i\mathbf r\cdot\partial_j\mathbf r$, the metric induced by the configuration. Rigid motions leave $g_{ij}$ unchanged, so the metric records exactly the deformation. If the body would be stress-free with lengths given by a reference metric $\bar g_{ij}$, the natural strain is $\varepsilon_{ij}=\tfrac12(g_{ij}-\bar g_{ij})$, the Green–Lagrange strain, and the elastic energy density is quadratic in it, with indices raised by a metric. For an ordinary body $\bar g_{ij}$ is the metric of its relaxed shape, but it can be any metric, even one that no shape in space realizes, as in growing tissue. The paper treats a viscoelastic body in these metric tensors: at each instant elastic, about a reference metric that slowly changes.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-lame", "after": "section-III-D-p2", "title": "Isotropic stiffness and the Poisson ratio", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>An isotropic linear elastic material has only two independent elastic constants. Its stiffness tensor can be written $C^{ijkl}=\lambda h^{ij}h^{kl}+2\mu h^{ik}h^{jl}$, where $h$ is the metric that defines rotations and $\lambda,\mu$ are the Lamé constants, $\mu$ being the shear modulus. The energy density $\tfrac12C^{ijkl}\varepsilon_{ij}\varepsilon_{kl}$ is then $\tfrac\lambda2(\operatorname{tr}\varepsilon)^2+\mu\operatorname{tr}\varepsilon^2$: one term penalizes changes of volume, the other changes of shape. An equivalent pair is Young's modulus $E$ and the Poisson ratio $\nu=\lambda/2(\lambda+\mu)$, the ratio of lateral contraction to axial extension of a stretched bar. As $\lambda/\mu\to\infty$, volume changes become infinitely costly, $\nu\to1/2$, and the material is incompressible; rubbers come close to this. For isotropic stiffness the paper's choice is which metric plays the role of $h$: it takes the rest reference metric.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-incompatible", "after": "section-V-p3", "title": "Non-Euclidean reference metrics and residual stress", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>Not every metric is the metric of some shape in space. A metric on a three-dimensional body comes from a configuration $\mathbf r(x)$ only if it is Euclidean, which means that its Riemann curvature tensor vanishes everywhere; these are the compatibility conditions of elasticity. A reference metric with curvature, called non-Euclidean or incompatible, cannot be realized: whatever shape the body takes, some pieces are stretched or compressed relative to their preferred lengths. The body then carries residual stress even with no external load, and is said to be geometrically frustrated. Such states arise in growing leaves and tissues, in swelling gels, and under non-uniform heating, and are the subject of non-Euclidean (incompatible) elasticity. The set of Euclidean metrics is not convex, so a mixture of two of them is generally non-Euclidean; this is why a reference metric relaxing between a rest metric and a held one can leave a frustrated state.</p>"""},
]
